#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063962 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e901ea71-aeab-5e95-939e-2401558aa708" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Global Extrema of a Function on a Given Interval"}] :question/id #uuid "69180c3b-7c26-5e5f-8d7a-42aed98431bc" :question/math-academy-id "e-3736" :question/problem "Find the global extrema of $f(x) = 2x - x^2$  on the interval $[-1,4].$" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=2x-x^2,$ we get\n\n$$\nf'(x) = 2-2x,\n$$\n\nand solving $f'(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n2-2x = 0 \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad x=1.\n$$\n\nThe critical points are $x=1$ and the endpoints $x=-1,4$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =2(-1)-(-1)^{2}=-3 \\\\ f(1) & =2(1)-(1)^{2}=1 \\\\ f(4) & =2(4)-(4)^{2}=-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(1)=1,$ so the global maximum value is $1.$\n\n- The least value is $f(4)=-8,$ so the global minimum value is $-8.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/assets/c277beb3423cc80d3ac2a001ac1063059b645c65fc8cb77978050f3831274b31.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063963 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2106ea68-f48f-5372-87c0-fa8126509655" :knowledge-point/title "Discarding Stationary Points of a Function on a Given Interval"}] :question/id #uuid "a0f47cd2-ea8f-5278-ab67-46ecd65d2f4d" :question/math-academy-id "e-3737" :question/problem "Find the global maximum and minimum values for the function $f(x) = x^4 - 6 x^2$ on the closed interval $[-1, 3].$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=x^4 - 6x^2,$ we get\n\n$$\nf'(x) = 4x^3 - 12x,\n$$\n\nand solving $f'(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3}-12x & =0 \\\\ 4x({x}^{2}-3) & =0 \\\\ 4x(x+\\sqrt{3})(x-\\sqrt{3}) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\pm \\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x = -\\sqrt 3 \\approx -1.73$ does not belong to the interval $[-1,3]$, we ignore this point.\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=0,\\sqrt{3}$ and the endpoints $x=-1,3$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\nFinally, we evaluate $f$ at the critical points:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\tf(-1) \t& = (-1)^4 - 6(-1)^2 = -5\\[5pt]\n \t\tf(0) \t\t& = (0)^4 - 6(0)^2 = 0\\[5pt]\n \t\tf(\\sqrt 3) \t& = (\\sqrt 3)^4 - 6(\\sqrt 3)^2 = -9\\[5pt]\n \t\tf(3) \t\t& = (3)^4 - 6(3)^2 = 27 \\\t\t\n \t\t}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the global maximum is $27$ and the global minimum is $-9.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/assets/74ce5114dbc93035b0be078be12149cced66ce881dfbc37768ed9febcdfcba87.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381694 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e901ea71-aeab-5e95-939e-2401558aa708" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Global Extrema of a Function on a Given Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "989a5a3f-5916-5042-b162-be319a856176" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381696} {:answer/id #uuid "d7823951-f480-54ea-84ad-915c859f00be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381697} {:answer/id #uuid "f81d1dd7-6348-5d1d-842a-7237ea7047a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381698} {:answer/id #uuid "93983b7f-c96c-5e1e-a7b4-a01bbbe09ff1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381699} {:answer/id #uuid "51e2cc7f-2b5c-5735-a9b9-f731a23b42fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381700}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93983b7f-c96c-5e1e-a7b4-a01bbbe09ff1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381699} :answer-field/id #uuid "37134555-49e1-5960-b941-1af48cca1283" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381695}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9a91b466-7c46-5f76-b16e-9a0cfca603bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-113769" :question/problem "Find the global minimum value of the function $f(x)=\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{{e}^{x}}$ on the closed interval $[-1,1].$" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{{e}^{x}},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{2(x+1){e}^{x}-(x+1)^{2}({e}^{x})}{{e}^{2x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x+2-{x}^{2}-2x-1}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{x}^{2}}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1-x)(1+x)}{{e}^{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=-1,1,$ which coincide with the endpoints of the given interval.\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =\\frac{(-1+1)^{2}}{{e}^{-1}}=0 \\\\ f(1) & =\\frac{(1+1)^{2}}{{e}^{1}}=\\frac{4}{e}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(1)=\\frac{4}{e},$ so the global maximum is $\\frac{4}{e}.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(-1)=0,$ so the global minimum is $0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/assets/e21d39f09f56fa1113184ce7a778d98561b82b932cd324ee90cf3aaff4d729d9.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381701 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e901ea71-aeab-5e95-939e-2401558aa708" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Global Extrema of a Function on a Given Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a4bf96e-7ac6-59a6-bab8-4631fcb3d9bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381703} {:answer/id #uuid "80c41a44-d3b4-5185-ac2d-a7ead1480144" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381704} {:answer/id #uuid "e26c594b-d766-549d-8475-f3151f2d0eae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381705} {:answer/id #uuid "50eb51f1-f87d-598d-86b9-78839b90cff5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381706} {:answer/id #uuid "5deaa947-60af-5427-80bc-7fde7faf3ed7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381707}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "50eb51f1-f87d-598d-86b9-78839b90cff5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381706} :answer-field/id #uuid "0577fe80-4ecc-52cc-9b93-316d000ed2ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381702}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0685e1c6-7326-51d7-82c2-83d6b3ede4ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-20952" :question/problem "Find the global maximum value of the function $f(x)=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}-1}$ on the closed interval $[3,5].$" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}-1},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{(2)({x}^{2}-1)-(2x)(2x)}{({x}^{2}-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}-2-4{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2-2{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2(1+{x}^{2})}{({x}^{2}-1)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ for all real values of $x,$ with $x\\ne \\pm 1.$ So there is no solution for ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$ \n\nSo, the only critical points are the endpoints $x=3,5$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3) & =\\frac{2(3)}{(3)^{2}-1}=\\frac{6}{8}=\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ f(5) & =\\frac{2(5)}{(5)^{2}-1}=\\frac{10}{24}=\\frac{5}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(3)=\\frac{3}{4},$ so the global maximum is $\\frac{3}{4}.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(5)=\\frac{5}{12},$ so the global minimum is $\\frac{5}{12}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/assets/14747ab1b46e589f0692d9e3dc5ffbe91eb1d60e3641d47d1a5eacb5f1df1130.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064528 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e901ea71-aeab-5e95-939e-2401558aa708" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Global Extrema of a Function on a Given Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90e11493-093d-5677-9cce-796074ba130e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061700} {:answer/id #uuid "c5d8e750-c89b-5ba7-b458-68ac026f484a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061701} {:answer/id #uuid "d85f6949-16e9-59c0-8f4b-cd4eba8557d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061702} {:answer/id #uuid "1d8ae605-3c4c-5bbe-bc7a-ba41af30bda2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061703} {:answer/id #uuid "7aec39cd-06b6-51e7-8fac-330d7d637bcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061704}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5d8e750-c89b-5ba7-b458-68ac026f484a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061701} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c6adffb-ec69-5386-895c-90f005cfeb37" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061699}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "542cb4a0-6380-5eaf-91e8-44f9300f10c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-22428" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=x-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ defined on the closed interval $\\frac{1}{2}\\le x\\le e,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The global maximum value is $\\frac{1}{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$\n- The global minimum value is $e-2$\n- The global minimum value is $2-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=x-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=1-\\frac{2}{x},\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n1-\\frac{2}{x}=0\\,⟹\\,x=2.\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=2,$ and the endpoints $x=\\frac{1}{2},e$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{1}{2}) & =\\frac{1}{2}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2≈1.9 \\\\ f(2) & =2-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2≈0.6 \\\\ f(e) & =e-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡e=e-2≈0.7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,$ so the global maximum is $\\frac{1}{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(2)=2-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,$ so the global minimum is $2-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is, \"I and III only.\"\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/assets/6cc3ee476c8c5dc9e27051c990b184318147caffd2177b760074172fe5c667f6.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381708 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2106ea68-f48f-5372-87c0-fa8126509655" :knowledge-point/title "Discarding Stationary Points of a Function on a Given Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ced4697d-765a-529d-a4dd-00f4dafdf98e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381710} {:answer/id #uuid "80fe567f-8313-57c8-a1e9-a987c3ad37de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381711} {:answer/id #uuid "d6ae36bc-22f7-5c23-a87e-5447737387ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381712} {:answer/id #uuid "5e21c718-0110-5de8-a336-f3fd0076f9ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381713} {:answer/id #uuid "cf668686-0ec1-5c92-85e4-4a0b1546ca2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381714}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ced4697d-765a-529d-a4dd-00f4dafdf98e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381710} :answer-field/id #uuid "c693db04-4e68-5288-bd55-043640c57719" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381709}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d3d1d570-9705-5d73-82f5-b8bee8639610" :question/math-academy-id "q-22615" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ defined on the closed interval $0\\le x\\le \\pi ,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The global maximum value is $\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\sqrt{3}$\n- The global maximum value is $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+\\sqrt{3}$\n- The global minimum value is $0$" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2\\pi n,\\,\\,\\,\\frac{4\\pi }{3}+2\\pi n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer. We discard all solutions except $x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ since it is the only value that belongs to the given interval.\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3},$ and the endpoints $x=0,\\pi $ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =0+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)=0 \\\\ f(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+\\sqrt{3}≈3.8 \\\\ f(\\pi ) & =\\pi +2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )=\\pi ≈3.1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+\\sqrt{3},$ so the global maximum is $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+\\sqrt{3}.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(0)=0,$ so the global minimum is $0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/assets/539dc4ccd2f1b20f521ae0f70bb5d95b8c5f9d1a006ff102baf43ac13ce19660.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381715 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2106ea68-f48f-5372-87c0-fa8126509655" :knowledge-point/title "Discarding Stationary Points of a Function on a Given Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dd40f474-1dd7-531e-a6b9-75f2cbd9d8c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381717} {:answer/id #uuid "bea2f439-8a1e-5005-acec-6526275fb0ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381718} {:answer/id #uuid "be573206-7ab8-5025-9584-395733e6cb27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381719} {:answer/id #uuid "0ae1905a-e5c1-5d29-a23d-2e5881b5d875" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381720} {:answer/id #uuid "f48dd62e-c1b7-5701-874f-e853928e35eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381721}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be573206-7ab8-5025-9584-395733e6cb27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381719} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9f2ee4e-8955-53e8-86ec-4364035cbbfe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381716}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d05c5e81-6be8-59d5-ab47-d0e86e875c97" :question/math-academy-id "q-2691" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)={x}^{4}-3{x}^{2}+4,$ on the closed interval $-1\\le x\\le 1,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The global maximum value is $4$\n- The global maximum value is $2$\n- The global minimum value is $0$" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)={x}^{4}-3{x}^{2}+4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =4{x}^{3}-6x \\\\  & =2x(2{x}^{2}-3),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n2x(2{x}^{2}-3)=0\\,⟹\\,x=0,\\,\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}.\n$$\n\nSince $x=\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}$ do not belong to the interval $[-1,1]$, the critical points are $x=0$ and the endpoints $x=-1,1$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =(-1)^{4}-3(-1)^{2}+4=2 \\\\ f(0) & =(0)^{4}-3(0)^{2}+4=4 \\\\ f(1) & =(1)^{4}-3(1)^{2}+4=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(0)=4,$ so the global maximum is $4.$\n\n- The least value is $f(-1)=f(1)=2,$ so the global minimum is $2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/assets/a7141a342d971d5b520b2f7f07f8fdb1c01ddca18a983fb0c83102a5bd4aee8e.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316022 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2106ea68-f48f-5372-87c0-fa8126509655" :knowledge-point/title "Discarding Stationary Points of a Function on a Given Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25d86cfd-3823-5e43-a83b-0971d6221498" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316024} {:answer/id #uuid "bebb93d0-8f13-5c5f-ab86-c1fde0abceb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381722}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25d86cfd-3823-5e43-a83b-0971d6221498" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316024} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3729fc4-3797-5340-81aa-57fda3329d71" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316023}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9641cbff-6df9-5810-96c4-dcfb71465c80" :question/math-academy-id "q-314729" :question/problem "The exact global minimum value of the function $f(x)=2{x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}+1$ on the closed interval $-1\\le x\\le 1$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=2{x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}+1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =8{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}(2x-3) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ does not belong to the interval $[-1,1],$ the critical points are $x=0$ and the endpoints $x=-1,1$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =2(-1)^{4}-4(-1)^{3}+1=2+4+1=7 \\\\ f(0) & =2(0)^{4}-4(0)^{3}+1=1 \\\\ f(1) & =2(1)^{4}-4(1)^{3}+1=2-4+1=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(-1)=7,$ so the global maximum is $7.$\n\n- The least value is $f(1)=-1,$ so the global minimum is $-1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/assets/fa4a3f723bb55521ec02dda8d088427b1939721366155f5ac45dd8e1429992e7.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381723 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2106ea68-f48f-5372-87c0-fa8126509655" :knowledge-point/title "Discarding Stationary Points of a Function on a Given Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15c7c45c-ad4a-575a-adc2-18e6e78bc781" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381725}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15c7c45c-ad4a-575a-adc2-18e6e78bc781" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381725} :answer-field/id #uuid "8776dca8-a97f-5180-8c28-51a7954bf1fd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381724}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5a631e09-e1d1-50ec-bbed-e44b79c9b773" :question/math-academy-id "q-344464" :question/problem "The exact global maximum value of the function $f(x)=\\sqrt{x+4}-x$ on the closed interval $-1\\le x\\le 2$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=\\sqrt{x+4}-x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+4}}-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+4}}-1 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+4}} & =1 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{x+4} & =1 \\\\ \\sqrt{x+4} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x+4 & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{15}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x=-\\frac{15}{4}$ does not belong to the interval $[-1,2],$ the critical points are the endpoints $x=-1,2$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =\\sqrt{-1+4}-(-1)=\\sqrt{3}+1 \\\\ f(2) & =\\sqrt{2+4}-2=\\sqrt{6}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(-1)=\\sqrt{3}+1,$ so the global maximum is $\\sqrt{3}+1.$\n\n- The least value is $f(2)=\\sqrt{6}-2,$ so the global minimum is $\\sqrt{6}-2.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/assets/ade599dd27bc3d018a3604620f3741ae05f8ebcc8ff583994da155ce7f311146.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381726 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2106ea68-f48f-5372-87c0-fa8126509655" :knowledge-point/title "Discarding Stationary Points of a Function on a Given Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a76a859a-c6f2-510e-a346-641b648f5445" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381728} {:answer/id #uuid "48d95e5e-390a-5265-a123-55f754215771" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381729}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a76a859a-c6f2-510e-a346-641b648f5445" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381728} :answer-field/id #uuid "17bc309a-4946-559c-8ac4-4407812e6d91" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381727}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "42ea67fe-58dc-59cf-90b1-7bed8af018d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-344477" :question/problem "The exact global maximum value of the function $f(x)=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ on the closed interval $0\\le x\\le \\pi $ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) & =0 \\\\ 2x & =\\pi n \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}n,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer. We discard all solutions except $x=0,$ $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ and $x=\\pi ,$ since they are the only values that belong to the given interval.\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=0,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\pi ,$ and the endpoints $x=0,\\,\\pi $ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=0 \\\\ f\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )=1-(-1)=2 \\\\ f(\\pi ) & =1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=2,$ so the global maximum is $2.$\n\n- The least value is $f(0)=0,$ so the global minimum is $0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/assets/6b8ac504d93007049795ee92eb8865225751314478d68ace67878e27ed471b24.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316031 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e901ea71-aeab-5e95-939e-2401558aa708" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Global Extrema of a Function on a Given Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb7c8a88-7f04-5f7e-9b7f-f4f5587b5967" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316033} {:answer/id #uuid "ca196cc7-5247-57ff-aab0-7f32005b1f39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316034} {:answer/id #uuid "c7c2d143-9fbc-55ff-b6d3-fb85b27cdf06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316035} {:answer/id #uuid "12b0d977-b3cb-5278-8cd6-1f04b91f46cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316036} {:answer/id #uuid "96a1a329-9bb4-5188-ae5d-6b9297be6feb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316037}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96a1a329-9bb4-5188-ae5d-6b9297be6feb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316037} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c9a0c81-1e18-52e2-b8f7-b3422105b226" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316032}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "941af544-ae4c-5a01-8911-5ecc21b5700c" :question/math-academy-id "q-79342" :question/problem "Find the global minimum value of the function $f(x)=-{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-1$ on the closed interval $[-1,3].$" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=-{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-3{x}^{2}+6x,\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{x}^{2}+6x & =0 \\\\ -3x(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=0,2,$ and the endpoints $x=-1,3$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =-(-1)^{3}+3(-1)^{2}-1=3 \\\\ f(0) & =-(0)^{3}+3(0)^{2}-1=-1 \\\\ f(2) & =-(2)^{3}+3(2)^{2}-1=3 \\\\ f(3) & =-(3)^{3}+3(3)^{2}-1=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(-1)=f(2)=3,$ so the global maximum is $3.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(0)=f(3)=-1,$ so the global minimum is $-1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/assets/ebc8acabf87ca5c3423f2f7c07637a87c5963d9cb080a4d52f9efd87846380d1.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316038 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e901ea71-aeab-5e95-939e-2401558aa708" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Global Extrema of a Function on a Given Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e1516c2-cc07-5c5f-b898-0990f23acb1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316040} {:answer/id #uuid "433119df-86a3-5468-9111-f82c4d0d2689" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316041} {:answer/id #uuid "8503c929-858e-5499-9966-338c4d064113" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316042} {:answer/id #uuid "4de72150-9af2-58c3-b28d-7d3a160b8362" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316043} {:answer/id #uuid "6b2d2a4f-fa93-59c6-9188-13be7147ccaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316044}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8503c929-858e-5499-9966-338c4d064113" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316042} :answer-field/id #uuid "200ef30a-708e-5701-8caa-a99a29a67914" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316039}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "31376917-58ed-53d3-897e-f420b4febe17" :question/math-academy-id "q-79348" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=-\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+1},$ defined on the closed interval $[-2,1],$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The global minimum value is $-1$\n- The global maximum value is $1$\n- The global maximum value is $\\frac{1}{2}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=-\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+1},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{1({x}^{2}+1)-x(2x)}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}+1-2{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)(x-1)}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x+1)(x-1)}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}}=0\\,⟹\\,x=\\pm 1.\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=\\pm 1,$ and the endpoints $x=-2,1$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =-\\frac{(-2)}{(-2)^{2}+1}=\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ f(-1) & =-\\frac{(-1)}{(-1)^{2}+1}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ f(1) & =-\\frac{1}{{1}^{2}+1}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(-1)=\\frac{1}{2},$ so the global maximum is $\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(1)=-\\frac{1}{2},$ so the global minimum is $-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14075289/assets/f3165b8b46c8ac0ab798ae6162d4b4facb92367f5072598bef936801bce44fc4.png)"}]}
